**[Suggestion]** The comment says "fail only when both are missing" — but `toolCall=true, updated=false` also fails (the inner `if (toolCall) { expect(updated).toBe(true) }` guard catches it). A future maintainer trusting this comment might remove the `if (toolCall)` block as seemingly redundant with the stated intent, which would let a detected-but-failed write pass silently.

Failure scenario: `write_file` is called but fails (wrong `old_string`, permissions), the developer removes the "redundant" `if (toolCall)` guard, and the test passes with `toolCall=true, updated=false` — masking a real write failure.

```suggestion
      // Tool call detection can miss real calls in docker/podman sandbox
      // (telemetry log flush races, stdout fallback in readToolLogs), and
      // the model may use run_shell_command instead of write_file/edit.
      // Correct file content is always required; a detected tool call is
      // optional.
```

